{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And I appeared to Abraham, etc.</b> It begins with the language of seeing and concludes with the language of knowing — for it should have said, 'And I appeared to Abraham, etc., and by My name HaShem I was not seen by them,' or it should have said, 'And I made Myself known to Abraham, etc., and My name HaShem I did not make known to them.' And what seems right to me to say on this is that the language of seeing indicates greater clarity of a thing than the language of knowing, for you may have knowledge of a thing that is recognized and made known through a sign by way of logical inference, in which error may fall, or by way of hearing, in which falsehood may fall; but a thing that a person sees eye to eye is best of all, for no error or falsehood can fall in something seen. And you shall know: the name Shaddai indicates the name by which the Holy One, blessed be He, said to His world 'enough (dai),' and the name by which He says 'enough' to their troubles, as Rashi explained on the verse 'and El Shaddai grant you mercy' (Genesis 43:14). And it is well known that the patriarchs were in pain all their days, or most of them: Abraham was cast into the fiery furnace, wandered as a stranger, and his wife was taken. And so with Isaac — moreover his wells were stopped up, and he was a stranger in the land. And Jacob, more plainly than all of them. And the Holy One, blessed be He, said 'enough' to all their troubles, and this is the thing that they saw with their own eyes — all the affliction that passed over them, and HaShem saved them by the name Shaddai and said 'enough' to their troubles. But the name HaShem, which indicates pure mercy without any admixture of evil — not only did they not see this with their own eyes, but it was not even made known to them by way of demonstrative knowledge, that I, HaShem, am good to all." "If so, it employed seeing and knowing in the manner of 'not only this but even that': for the evils — not only knowing, but even seeing eye to eye were they shown to them; and the pure mercies — not only did they not see them yet, but even 'I did not make Myself known to them' (Exodus 6:3) by way of demonstrative knowledge. And yet I said to the patriarchs 'I am HaShem,' full of mercy, and I did not yet fulfill for them this promise — and this is the first sign concerning the redemption and the recompense. Second sign: 'and I also established My covenant with them to give them the land of Canaan' (Exodus 6:4), and yet I have not yet given it to them. Third sign: 'and I have also heard the groan of the children of Israel, whom Egypt enslaves' (Exodus 6:5) — labor harder than what was decreed, for 'I was angry a little and they helped to do harm' (Zechariah 1:15). These three joined together into the depth of the three promises that are mentioned afterward in three consecutive verses, for in them it is explicitly mentioned: 'Therefore say to the children of Israel, I am HaShem,' etc. (Exodus 6:6), and He mentioned great judgments to execute upon their oppressors, and entry into the land, as is clear to every discerning eye in these verses." "A second approach to the continuation of these verses is that 'Elohim' means nothing but 'judge,' and He did not yet specify in what judgment the Holy One, blessed be He, judged. Therefore it seems to me that this judgment refers to the verse before it, which says 'Now you shall see what I will do to Pharaoh' (Exodus 6:1). Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Sanhedrin 111a): 'and it was not done for the kings of seven nations.' Now, the word 'for the kings of' appears superfluous, for it should have said 'and it was not done to seven nations.' And in the Yalkut it concludes: 'and you will not see what I will do in the war against thirty-one kings.' And the difficulty is: why do I need a count? Do we not yet know that Israel fought against thirty-one kings? And furthermore, what is the meaning of what is said, 'Now you shall see what I will do to Pharaoh' — why do I need 'now'? Although at the end of parashat Shemot I explained the expression 'now,' nevertheless Elohim still has interpretations to explain it in different language, and this is it: for the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses, 'I am HaShem' — He was, is, and will be. For every flesh and blood is not guaranteed to fulfill his promise, for 'his spirit passes and he is not' (Psalms 103:16) to fulfill his promise; therefore he must fulfill it immediately. And if not, then those who were promised justly cry out against the fact that the word of his mouth was not carried out speedily — for if not now, when? But I, HaShem, am existent forever, in all times equally; therefore the patriarchs did not cry out, but rather they hoped and waited for when the word of HaShem would come, and they trusted that in the end it would be fulfilled. But you cried out because My words were not fulfilled immediately — if so, all the more so would you cry out concerning the wars against thirty-one kings, for those wars would necessarily be prolonged, as it is written (Joshua 11:18), 'Joshua made war against all these kings for many days,' for Israel could not conquer the whole land speedily lest the beasts of the field multiply against them, but rather little by little they would be driven out before them. And since your way is to cry out when the promise is not carried out speedily, therefore I decree upon you that you justly shall not see the prolonged war that will be against thirty-one kings, apart from the war against Pharaoh, who is one king — and you are able to see 'now,' that is to say immediately, what I will do to Pharaoh; that alone you shall see. And the essence of the restriction comes from the word 'now,' meaning specifically a thing that is done now, immediately, that you shall see — but not a thing that will not be done now but only after many days, like the war against thirty-one kings — that you shall not see. And this is what He said: 'but My name HaShem' — which indicates that I am existent forever, and My promise will ultimately be fulfilled; but they did not yet know this thing, and they waited, looking forward to My word as aforementioned." "A third approach is that the Holy One, blessed be He, wanted to proclaim in the world that He, blessed be He, created His world something from nothing, for miracles demonstrating the new creation were not yet widespread in the world. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, needed to perform all these miracles, so that they would believe retroactively that HaShem created His world something from nothing, as it is written 'By this you shall know that I am HaShem' (Exodus 7:17), and likewise Jethro said (Exodus 18:11), 'Now I know that HaShem is greater than all the elohim.' And many commentators spoke at length about this, how the miracles of Egypt were miracles demonstrating the new creation of the world. Therefore He said, 'And I appeared to the patriarchs as El Shaddai' — for this is a name indicating that the Holy One, blessed be He, said to His world 'enough (dai),' and it is a miracle concerning creation, but not concerning something from nothing. This is what He said: 'but My name HaShem I did not make known to them,' for this name of Being indicates that the Holy One, blessed be He, brings all into being, and all existences are from Him, blessed be He — for this validates the new creation, to say that there is no independent existence for any being besides Him, blessed be He. And this is a reply to Moses, who cried out and said, 'Why have You done evil,' etc. (Exodus 5:22), and even though HaShem had already told him, 'and he will not let you go' (Exodus 3:19), nevertheless Moses wanted to know the reason for the hardening of Pharaoh's heart to such a degree. And if it is in order to perform all the miracles, the question itself is difficult: what need is there for all the miracles, until the Holy One, blessed be He, told him the reason for the thing — for 'I am HaShem,' who brings all into being, and this matter has not yet been proclaimed in the world, for to the patriarchs I did not appear except by the name Shaddai, which indicates that I said to My world 'enough,' but a miracle demonstrating the new creation was not yet proclaimed. This is what He said: 'but My name HaShem I did not make known to them.' Therefore I need to harden Pharaoh's heart in order to perform miracles demonstrating the new creation." "And if you say: what distinguishes this day from other days, and what need is there for this knowledge now more than in other times? Concerning this He said: 'and I also established My covenant with them to give them the land of Canaan.' And if I do not proclaim now that to Me belongs all the earth, then the nations will say: 'You are robbers,' for with a mighty hand you conquer the lands of seven nations, and HaShem did not manifest among them (1 Samuel 3:13). And through this they will come to heresy and denial, to say: 'There is no judgment and no Judge,' as was explained above in parashat Bereshit (Genesis 1:1) in the name of Rabbi Yitzchak. And if you say: did I not also promise them in the days of the patriarchs to give them the land, and I did not make a proclamation concerning the new creation? Concerning this He said: 'and I have also heard the groan of the children of Israel,' etc. — meaning that the patriarchs did not need miracles, for in any case they were trustworthy sons; and as for all the nations, they had no need for this knowledge yet. But now that I have heard the groan of the children of Israel and the time has come for their entry into the land, therefore I need to proclaim now, by way of the miracles, that I am HaShem who brings all into being, and to Me belongs all the earth, and it is in My hand to give the land to whomever I wish. And this is what He said: 'and I will give it to you as a heritage, I am HaShem' — since I am HaShem, who brings all into being, and I made the earth and all that is upon it, therefore it is in My hand to give it to you as a heritage." "A fourth approach is along the lines of what the Sages said (Sanhedrin 90b): From where do we derive the resurrection of the dead from the Torah? For it says, ״וְגַם הֲקִימֹתִי אֶת בְּרִיתִי אִתָּם לָתֵת לָהֶם אֶת אֶרֶץ כְּנָעַן״ — it is not written 'to you' (לָכֶם) but 'to them' (לָהֶם); from here is derived the resurrection of the dead from the Torah. Rashi explained: from here, that they are destined to live again and inherit the land. And his interpretation is forced from several difficulties, for what has the resurrection of the dead to do with this account that HaShem had with Moses over his having picked a quarrel? And furthermore, we do not find anywhere in the Torah, the Prophets, or the Writings that in the days of the Messiah the patriarchs will come back to life and dwell on the soil of Israel, for what benefit would they have in this — to exchange a passing world for a world that is passing away? All the more so at the time of resurrection itself, for then the world will lie in ruins, so how would the patriarchs dwell upon the land? And there is a further difficulty against the midrash that Rashi cites (on verse 9): ״חֲבָל עַל דְּאָבְדִין״ etc. — 'I have much to complain about regarding the death of the patriarchs. I said to Abraham: Arise, walk about the land, through its length and breadth, for to you I will give it; yet he sought a place to bury Sarah and did not find one until he purchased it with money. And so with Isaac — they disputed over the wells. And so with Jacob, who needed to purchase the field' etc. — 'and he did not harbor any doubts.' Now the difficulty is straightforward: if the promise of the land was specifically for their seed and not for the patriarchs, then why would the patriarchs have harbored doubts and lodged complaints — for HaShem had never promised the patriarchs that they would have a share in the land. And if He did promise the patriarchs as well, then where is that promise?'" "And it seems closer to me to say that they taught it from two sides. For the patriarchs were not given a share in the land for the needs of this world, but only a burial place — for the dead of the land of Israel are alive first. And wherever it says ״כִּי לְךָ וּלְזַרְעֲךָ אֶתֵּן אֶת כָּל הָאֲרָצֹת הָאֵל״ (Genesis 26:3), the giving is not equal between the two, for how could individuals inherit so great a land? Rather, for their seed — in this world; and for them — for the needs of the resurrection of the dead in the world to come, that they would have a portion and burial in the land in order to escape the distress of the cycle of graves. If so, when Abraham sought a place to bury Sarah and did not find one, Abraham did have reason to harbor doubts about the very essence of this promise to the patriarchs — which was that they would have a burial place, and he did not find one. And in any case this promise was fulfilled afterward when he found an opportunity to purchase with money, but before he found an opportunity to purchase he did have reason to harbor doubts, and he did not harbor them. And so Isaac, when they disputed over the wells — he too had reason to harbor doubts, lest they not give him even a burial place in the land, since they scrutinize his every move so as not to give him even a meager share in the land. And so too Jacob." "And the matter of the resurrection of the dead depends on the creation of the world anew, for one who believes in creation will also believe in resurrection all the more so, as is found in the Gemara (Sanhedrin 91a): one who was not and then was — one who was is all the more certain to be. And this is what it says: ״וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים אֶל מֹשֶׁה וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו אֲנִי ה׳״ (Exodus 6:2) — He who brings all into being, who was and will also be in the future, and all of this is a demonstration of the resurrection. Therefore I needed to perform all the signs against Pharaoh and against all his servants, so that they would also believe in the resurrection — for until now, My name YHWH, which indicates creation anew, I did not make known to them. Yet even so: I established My covenant with them to give them the land of Canaan — 'to them,' that is, that they would be buried in the land so that they would be assured of standing in the world to come without the distress of the cycle of graves. And this interpretation fits correctly, whether according to the third approach — which reads the entire passage gently, not as those who blame Moses for having picked a quarrel — or according to the approach of Rashi, who explained ״חֲבָל עַל דְּאָבְדִין״ etc." "And it further appears to me that He mentioned the name Shaddai to the patriarchs in the promise of the land, to hint to them that the benefit of the land is in this world and in the world to come, for י״ש of Shaddai is a hint to the reward of the world to come, as it says (Proverbs 8:21): ״לְהַנְחִיל אֹהֲבַי יֵשׁ״. The ד׳ of Shaddai hints to the reward of this world, which spreads to the four winds of the world, as it is written (Genesis 28:14): ״וּפָרַצְתָּ יָמָּה וָקֵדְמָה״ etc. And concerning this it was said to Moses (Exodus 33:23): ״וְרָאִיתָ אֶת אֲחֹרָי וּפָנַי לֹא יֵרָאוּ״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, showed Moses the knot of the tefillin, for the דָּלֶ״ת of Shaddai is in the knot of the tefillin at the back, which the Holy One, blessed be He, showed him — all the reward of this world hinted at in the four winds, as it is said (Deuteronomy 3:27) to Moses: ״עֲלֵה רֹאשׁ הַפִּסְגָּה וְשָׂא עֵינֶיךָ יָמָּה צָפֹנָה״ etc. But ״פָּנַי״ — that is, the letters י״ש that are in the tefillin at the front, hinting to the reward of the world to come, namely שִׁי״ן in the tefillin of the head and יוֹ״ד in the tefillin of the arm — ״לֹא יֵרָאוּ״: He told him that the reward of the world to come shall not be seen, as it is written (Isaiah 64:3): ״עַיִן לֹא רָאָתָה אֱלֹהִים זוּלָתְךָ״. Therefore He mentioned here the name Shaddai, to say that they did not harbor doubts over not finding a burial place in the land — which comes for the purpose of resurrection — even though they were also promised the resurrection through the name Shaddai."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:19:20.208687924Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}